{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Audit complete. The corrected guidance matches rebar's documentation policy only when concision is judged against the artifact's reader, purpose, and lifecycle; it is not a universal ticket-length rule.\n\nProject mapping:\n- Current-state internal docs, the docs index, shipped help, templates, and code comments normally follow it: correct in place, lead with the usable contract, cite rather than copy history, and route detail progressively (docs/documentation-policy.md:13-16,22,30; docs/README.md:60-141).\n- AGENTS.md partially follows. Its declared design is lean launch context plus one-line pointers (lines 10-15,181-250), but the current 509-line file also embeds dated incident narrative and operator-recovery detail (252-330). Exact high-risk commands and repeated point-of-action safety constraints are justified; bulky history is a future move/link candidate.\n- Executable ticket descriptions/plans normally follow directly: short problem, one chosen path, grounded scope, measurable criteria, and verification (src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md:75-90,118-138). The line-241 “weigh alternatives” coaching move is temporary planner/reviewer reasoning: the G6 rubric explicitly says generate and DISCARD alternatives and retain one implementation path (src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_G6.md:10). Hard-to-reverse rationale belongs in an ADR.\n- Ticket comments/handoffs follow when they report only a delta, proof, blocker, decision, and exact next action. The compiled ticket intentionally retains corrections because events are append-only; retained history is not bloat, repeated recap is.\n- ADRs intentionally diverge: they retain context, serious alternatives, rejection reasons, consequences, reversibility, and supersession. docs/adr/0018-plan-review-trust-boundary-scope-gate.md:31-92 is representative.\n- User/operator docs follow the general minimum-sufficient contract but not ticket terseness. They need prerequisites, ordered steps, examples, expected results, environment alternatives, warnings at the point of risk, and likely-failure troubleshooting. docs/user-guide.md:11-75 demonstrates the primary path; docs/dependency-advisory-runbook.md:47-105 deliberately preserves failed remedies to prevent unsafe/costly rediscovery.\n- Reference/API docs intentionally diverge from ticket density: arbitrary-entry readers need complete parameters, defaults, errors, compatibility, edge cases, and some local repetition. Many are generated from canonical registries and must be corrected through their generators, not hand-compressed (docs/generated-artifacts.md:1-31; docs/mcp-reference.md:1-35).\n- Security material splits by role. docs/security.md is generated current reference; the dependency-advisory page is a living runbook. Actual incident/security evidence should retain chronology, exact safe errors, hypotheses, negative results, mitigations, hashes, and residual risk.\n- Historical validation/protected evidence intentionally diverges. docs/88ab-feature-branch-evidence.md:1-80 repeats change IDs, run IDs, pre/post state, and criterion-level proof because reproducibility is the purpose. Corrections are adjacent, not destructive.\n- session_log and research notes intentionally diverge. session_log is defined as “verbose, durable, agent-facing,” lifecycle/gate exempt, hidden from normal work queues, and never Jira-synced (docs/event-schema.md:146-177). It should have a concise summary/index, but may retain chronology, hypotheses, and raw evidence.\n- Licenses, quotations, captures, frozen output, serialized fixtures, generated outputs, historical ticket events, ADR substance, and protected evidence are excluded from ordinary prose rewriting (docs/documentation-policy.md:69-93).\n\nObserved ticket practice is mixed but supports this classification. In the deliberately long 20-ticket sample, 14 were appropriate and 6 bloated. The six defects were current-layer defects (revision residue, cross-section echo, or truncated acceptance residue), not mere length. S1-S3 perfectly fit that discovery sample but remain retrospective, not production-approved.\n\nDefect boundary: detail is bloat when it serves the wrong lifecycle or repeats retrievable material; it is necessary when it enables execution, decision revisit, user success, arbitrary-entry lookup, safety, audit, or reproduction.\n\nArtifacts:\n- session_log 59a7-cb88-f2f9-4148 (this audit appended)\n- this ticket comment\n- final user answer\n- no repository files"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788499590905461001,"uuid":"3497cffe-3ac5-4712-944a-9ce5495b27bc"}